// Services: what `spawn`, a send, a reply and `shutdown` lower to. A service is a cown whose value is its state, with its
// mailbox's gate after the cown's words (serviceGateOffset), and each message is a behaviour on that one cown, so the
// cown's queue is the mailbox and two messages of one service never run at once, preempted or not: a behaviour holds its
// cown until its thunk releases it. The release hands the next message to the green thread that ran the last, which runs
// up to a turn of them (runTurn), so an idle service holds no green thread and a busy one one. The gate counts the
// messages a send has queued and the service has not started, as maxon's does, and holds the service's flags above them.
// A mailbox has no capacity: a send fails only once the service is shut down.
// A reply is a cell shaped like a green thread that is awaited (greenAwaited), which never runs: the message's behaviour
// gives it its value and error as a coroutine's finish would, so an await of it is an await of a coroutine.
//
// Backpressure is Pony's (ponyc's actor.c): the runtime slows the senders of a service that falls behind, so overload
// moves upstream to where it can stop, and no program chooses a number.
//
// - A service slows its senders while it is behind: when a send finds a turn's worth (turnBehaviours) of its messages
//   queued, counting its own; when a turn of its messages ends at turnBehaviours with messages still queued (ponyc's
//   batch_limit_reached), until a turn empties its queue; and while it is muted. The count and both flags are in the gate
//   that every send compare-exchanges already, below and above the count, so one comparison of the word the send took its
//   place with against turnBehaviours decides it: a send to a service that is not behind costs what a send cost before.
//   The length rule bounds the queue in messages, whatever the speeds; the turn rule slows senders sooner where the
//   service runs whole turns without catching up.
// - A slowed sender waits until the receiver has started the messages counted ahead of it as it was slowed, its mark, or
//   catches up, which is when a turn empties its queue; not until the receiver falls back under a turn's worth, so a
//   sender that alone outruns a receiver is slowed once per backlog it finds, never on every message. A mark is the count
//   of the receiver's started messages plus the gate's places that are queued or on their way and the posted messages
//   queued (slowedMark), taken under the lock of the receiver's list, and a start that brings the started count to the
//   oldest mark lets go, in the order they were slowed, the senders whose marks it has passed (startCounted,
//   takeProgress). So a service that never gets ahead of its own posted messages (timers re-armed in their handlers
//   faster than it handles them, readiness) still lets a sender go once it has worked through what was ahead of it, where
//   an empty queue never comes. The receiver stays behind until its queue empties, so underLoad, the overload policies and
//   sheds see it behind for as long, and its senders are slowed at each send. A receiver that is listed on another lets
//   its own senders go only by catching up or by its own mute, as it drains on (the bounds below), and a listed service's
//   send to the receiver it is listed on moves its mark to that send's (markAgain), so it is let go only once the
//   receiver has started every message it sent while listed.
// - A service that sends to one that is behind is listed on it and muted at the end of a handler: the behaviour's release
//   is kept, so the service holds its cown and runs no next message, nothing waits inside a handler, and no green thread
//   is held, only the behaviour's record (behaviourRelease). A task, a green thread that runs no message (main, a
//   coroutine, a `when` that is no message of a service), parks at the send instead, even where nothing is counted ahead
//   of it, so that a listed receiver's task waits for its mute and cannot refill a drain one message at a time.
// - A listed service that is behind itself, by its turns or the length of its queue, or has senders waiting on it, drains
//   on until it holds no message, and is muted then (drainsOn): it is not stopped while it is overloaded (ponyc mutes a
//   sender only if it is not overloaded itself), but its own senders are slowed, so what it passes on is what it holds,
//   and it cannot pass on a refill. One that drains on by its length is made behind there (drainsBehind), so its
//   senders stay slowed through the drain, as they would not once its start took it under a turn's worth.
// - A service that lets its senders go unmutes them in the order they were muted, a turn's worth at a time onto the global
//   run queue, the last of each batch carrying the rest, so a fan-in is not woken all at once to overload it again
//   (ponyc #2980). There is no operation that applies pressure by hand, whose cycles deadlocked ponyc (#3382).
//
// The bound, in messages, is of the places a service's gate counts, its sent messages; its queue holds besides the posted
// messages outstanding, which take no place in the gate and are never slowed, and which their owners bound: one for each
// armed timer or ticker, each socket's arm and each watch of a stop. The posted ones only move marks later. The arguments
// below are of the sent ones; T is turnBehaviours.
//
// What a release of a slowed sender leaves (property L): a sender queues each message before its next send, a task's
// slowed one once it is let go and a service's at once, so the messages it sent before the slowed one were queued when it
// took the lock to be listed, and the ones queued then are the first of those its mark counts to start. So a sender let
// go by its mark, or by an empty queue, has every message it sent before the slowed one started. A sender let go by its
// receiver's own mute (muteService) or stop at once has no such promise.
//
// Tasks only, to a service that sends to none, so is never muted: max(T, tasks) + tasks. Let s be the last time a send
// went on unparked: unslowed, which finds fewer than T and no flag, or slowed and found caught up, which is fewer than T
// and no flag under the lock, so the count at s is at most T. Every send after s parks its
// task, and by L a task let go from the park of its slowed message r has every message before r started, so of those it
// sent after s at most two have not started, r and the next it parks with, and if two have not, none of its places
// counted at s is left. And when some task holds two, every place counted at s that has not started was still on its
// way when that task parked, the queued ones having started before it was let go: a task's place unqueued, at most one a
// task. So either every task holds at most one place sent after s, and the count is at most T + tasks, or every task
// holds at most two in all, 2 * tasks.
//
// A message on its way to the queue as its sender's mark is passed, which posted messages queued after the mark may have
// overtaken, is inside each bound below with no term of its own: L is of the sender's own earlier messages, which were
// queued before its mark was taken, and the bounds count each sender's one place on its way already. Here it is a task's
// place unqueued, of the at most one a task.
//
// A relay, which one task alone sends to: T + 2. With s as above, the count at s is at most T, and each of the task's
// sends r1 ... rb after s parks it; let e(j) be the release of the park of rj. Where some e(j) before e(b) is one with L,
// and e(q) the last of them, every message before rq has started, the releases e(q+1) ... e(b-1) are the relay's mutes,
// and between two of its mutes the relay runs a handler of a message started after e(q): at most 3 of rq ... rb have
// not started. Where none is, at least b - 2 of the messages counted at s or sent after it started after e(1), so at
// most T + 2 have not. The one task's place on its way is one of rq ... rb, or of those counted at s.
//
// The consumer that such a relay alone sends to, one message a handler, the relay with no posted messages of its own:
// 2 * T + 4 (2 * T + 3 derives). The consumer sends to none, so is never muted, and lets the relay go only by its mark,
// which markAgain keeps at the relay's last send while listed, or by an empty queue: L for each send but that last. Let s
// be the last time the relay sent unlisted, finding fewer than T and no flag, so at most T counted then. Where the relay
// is let go after s, the last such release leaves at most its last message unstarted, on its way (overtaken by posted
// messages, or an empty queue found before it was queued), and every send after it is listed: the count is at most 1 + E.
// Where it is not, every send after s is of one listing: at most T + E. E is the relay's sends in one listing, one a
// handler: the handler h0 that listed it, and, as long as it drains on, those it runs after; muted, it sends no more until
// let go. At the end of h0 it drains on only if it is behind, holds T (drainsBehind then makes it behind), or has its
// task parked on it. Behind, every send of the task from then on is slowed, sets it parked, and it is not let go while the
// relay is listed: the relay's progress lets nobody go while it is listed (startCounted), it does not empty while listed
// (it mutes then), and its mute ends E. Parked, the task stays so for the same reasons. So the relay runs in the listing
// only what was counted on it as h0 ended, less the task's place parked: at most its T + 2. E is at most 1 + (T + 2), and
// the count at most 2 * T + 3, with the message on its way inside it as 1 is at most T.
//
// A chain adds a stage's bound at each stage. A chunk of a parallel loop runs on a task of its own, so its parts count as
// tasks. A service is listed on one receiver at a time, so a second receiver that is behind gets that service's next
// handler after each unmute by the first, and its bound counts on how often the first lets it go. A cycle whose handlers
// send more than they take grows, which no bound in messages could stop without stopping the program. A kind of a
// coalescing policy adds one message to each bound, its pending word's, which its sends queue without being slowed; a kind
// whose policy refuses or discards adds none.
//
// Why muting never stops the program, with no service, task or behaviour that can run (the progress argument): a sender
// is listed on a receiver under the receiver's lock while the receiver slows senders, with a mark that counts only
// messages queued or on their way to the queue that wait on nobody (slowedMark leaves out the places of parked tasks),
// so a receiver that runs reaches every mark; and its catching up takes its whole list under that lock. A receiver
// listed on another lets its senders go when it catches up or is muted, and while it drains on it runs. So every entry
// is unmuted once its receiver has run through what was ahead of it, caught up or been muted. A receiver that
// is not muted runs, since a handler never waits on another service. So a muted sender could wait for ever only in a
// cycle of muted services, each listed on the next for good. A service is muted under its own lock, which lets go every
// sender listed on it so far (muteService), so whoever stays listed on a muted service was listed after its mute, and was
// itself muted later still, when its handler ended. Around a cycle each mute would come after the next one's: none
// closes, whatever the rule that made the receivers behind. Tasks wait on no one but a receiver. A muted behaviour stays
// counted among the live ones, so the program does not end before it has run.

// The gate's flags, above every count of messages: serviceClosed once the service is shut down, serviceBehind while a
// turn ended at turnBehaviours with messages queued, serviceMuted while a message of the service waits, its release kept,
// for a service it sent to, and serviceStoppedNow, with serviceClosed, once stopNow ended it. serviceQueued masks the count
// of places, in the low half; serviceParked, between the two, counts those of them that are the places of slowed tasks that
// have not queued their messages yet, a serviceParkedUnit each, which a slowed task's own compare-exchange of the gate adds
// with its place (serviceReserve) and the lock holder that lets it go takes off (ringTaken, takeProgress). serviceSlowing
// masks the flags and the count, which is what is compared with turnBehaviours.
let serviceClosed = 0x4000000000000000 as Count

let serviceBehind = 0x2000000000000000 as Count

let serviceMuted = 0x1000000000000000 as Count

let serviceStoppedNow = 0x0800000000000000 as Count

let serviceQueued = 0x00000000ffffffff as Count

let serviceParked = 0x07ffffff00000000 as Count

let serviceParkedUnit = 0x0000000100000000 as Count

let serviceSlowing = 0x78000000ffffffff as Count

// noMark is a service's serviceReleaseAtOffset while no sender is slowed on it: no count of starts reaches it.
let noMark = 0xffffffffffffffff as Count

// The words rt.service.reserve gives: a place taken, or the case of SendError plus 1 (sendServiceStopped of the
// built-ins).
let sendAccepted = 0 as Count

let sendStopped = 1 as Count

// noService is a green thread's service while it runs no message (greenServiceOffset).
let noService = 0 as Count

// A list of slowed senders (serviceMutedOffset) is a ring of entries: a service plus senderService, linked through its
// serviceLinkOffset, or a task's green thread, linked through its run queue link, which nothing reads while it is parked.
// mutedLock is added to the ring's word while one holds it; an address is a multiple of a word, so neither tag is part of
// one.
let noSender = 0 as Count

let senderService = 2 as Count

let mutedLock = 1 as Count

// What a service holds in its serviceWaitOffset besides the record of its kept message: nothing, or that it is listed on a
// service that slows it.
let waitNone = 0 as Count

let waitListed = 1 as Count

// behaviourRelease's answers to a behaviour's thunk: the cowns are handed on, or kept while the behaviour is muted.
let releaseHandedOn = 0 as Count

let releaseHeld = 1 as Count

// serviceCreate is a service of one owner with no message queued, holding state: the state the factory gave when it ran on
// the spawner, or 0 until the behaviour that runs the factory on the service stores it.
function serviceCreate(state Count) returns Count
	let svc = alloc(serviceBytes)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: cownCountOffset, value: firstOwner)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: cownTailOffset, value: notQueued)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: cownEndingOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: cownValueOffset, value: state)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceGateOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceHandledOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceReleaseAtOffset, value: noMark)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceMutedOffset, value: noSender)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceMostOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceLinkOffset, value: noSender)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceWaitOffset, value: waitNone)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceArmedOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: servicePostedOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceMarkOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceOnOffset, value: noService)
	return svc
end 'serviceCreate'

// serviceReserve takes a place in the mailbox for a message, by a compare-exchange of the gate, unless the service is shut
// down. The gate is read once, as an acquire, and then by each compare-exchange that fails, so a send takes one locked
// operation on the gate when no other send races it. The word it took the place with slows the sender when its flags and
// count are turnBehaviours or more: a flag below serviceClosed, or that many messages queued before this one. A slowed
// task, which parks with its place taken and its message not queued, adds a serviceParkedUnit in the same exchange, so
// that whoever reads the gate sees the place and that it is not queued at once (slowedMark).
function serviceReserve(handle Count) returns Count
	var gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	var parks = false
	var asked = false
	while true 'gate'
		if gate >= serviceClosed 'closed'
			return sendStopped
		end 'closed'
		let slowed = (gate and serviceSlowing) >= turnBehaviours
		var desired = __Raw.wrappingAdd(gate, right: 1)
		if slowed 'slowed'
			if not asked 'ask'
				parks = sendParks()
				asked = true
			end 'ask'
			if parks 'parks'
				desired = __Raw.wrappingAdd(desired, right: serviceParkedUnit)
			end 'parks'
		end 'slowed'
		let seen = __Raw.compareExchange(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset, expected: gate, desired: desired)
		if seen == gate 'taken'
			if slowed 'behind'
				noteQueued(handle, queued: __Raw.wrappingAdd(gate and serviceQueued, right: 1))
				senderSlowed(handle, parks: parks)
			end 'behind'
			return sendAccepted
		end 'taken'
		gate = seen
	end 'gate'
	return sendAccepted
end 'serviceReserve'

// Overload policies (README, "Overload policies"). A send of a kind whose type names a policy other than Keep takes its
// place by serviceTryReserve, which is serviceReserve while the service is not behind, so such a send costs what any other
// does then. While it is behind, the send takes no place and asks the policy's admit, which the compiler calls directly,
// and does what it answers: queue (serviceReserve, which slows the sender), refuse or discard (nothing is taken), or, for a
// kind of a CoalescingPolicy, replace the pending one (servicePlace, servicePutPending). A service whose type has such kinds
// holds a pending word for each after its own words (serviceCreatePending): the box of the arguments of the one message of
// the kind sent while it was behind that has not run, or 0. Each word that goes from 0 to a box has one behaviour queued for
// it, which takes the box (serviceTakePending), so a send that finds a box there replaces it and gives its place back. A
// send of the kind that finds a box there replaces it whether the service is behind or not (servicePendingHeld), since the
// box was sent before it and its behaviour is queued ahead: a sender's messages of the kind run in the order it sent them.
let sendBehind = 3 as Count

function servicePendingHeld(handle Count, slot Count) returns bool
	return __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: pendingOffset(slot)) != 0
end 'servicePendingHeld'

function serviceCreatePending(state Count, words Count) returns Count
	let svc = alloc(pendingOffset(words))
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: cownCountOffset, value: firstOwner)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: cownTailOffset, value: notQueued)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: cownEndingOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: cownValueOffset, value: state)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceGateOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceHandledOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceReleaseAtOffset, value: noMark)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceMutedOffset, value: noSender)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceMostOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceLinkOffset, value: noSender)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceWaitOffset, value: waitNone)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceArmedOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: servicePostedOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceMarkOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: serviceOnOffset, value: noService)
	var i = 0 as Count
	while i < words 'words'
		__Raw.storeWord(svc, offset: pendingOffset(i), value: 0)
		i = __Raw.wrappingAdd(i, right: 1)
	end 'words'
	return svc
end 'serviceCreatePending'

function pendingOffset(slot Count) returns Count
	return try serviceBytes + slot * wordBytes otherwise panic("a pending word past memory")
end 'pendingOffset'

// serviceTryReserve is serviceReserve while the service is not behind; while it is, it takes no place and gives sendBehind.
function serviceTryReserve(handle Count) returns Count
	var gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	while true 'gate'
		if gate >= serviceClosed 'closed'
			return sendStopped
		end 'closed'
		if (gate and serviceSlowing) >= turnBehaviours 'behind'
			return sendBehind
		end 'behind'
		let seen = __Raw.compareExchange(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset, expected: gate, desired: __Raw.wrappingAdd(gate, right: 1))
		if seen == gate 'taken'
			return sendAccepted
		end 'taken'
		gate = seen
	end 'gate'
	return sendAccepted
end 'serviceTryReserve'

function serviceQueuedCount(handle Count) returns Count
	return __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset) and serviceQueued
end 'serviceQueuedCount'

function serviceTurn() returns Count
	return turnBehaviours
end 'serviceTurn'

// servicePlace takes a place for the message of a pending word, unless the service is shut down, and never slows the
// sender: the message replaces any other of its kind that waits.
function servicePlace(handle Count) returns Count
	var gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	while true 'gate'
		if gate >= serviceClosed 'closed'
			return sendStopped
		end 'closed'
		let seen = __Raw.compareExchange(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset, expected: gate, desired: __Raw.wrappingAdd(gate, right: 1))
		if seen == gate 'taken'
			return sendAccepted
		end 'taken'
		gate = seen
	end 'gate'
	return sendAccepted
end 'servicePlace'

function serviceUnplace(handle Count)
	_ = __Raw.atomicAdd(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset, value: stepDown)
end 'serviceUnplace'

function servicePutPending(handle Count, slot Count, box Count) returns Count
	return __Raw.atomicExchange(handle, offset: pendingOffset(slot), value: box)
end 'servicePutPending'

function serviceTakePending(state Count, slot Count) returns Count
	let handle = __Raw.wrappingSub(state, right: cownValueOffset)
	let box = __Raw.atomicExchange(handle, offset: pendingOffset(slot), value: 0)
	if box == 0 'empty'
		panic("a pending message's behaviour found its word empty")
	end 'empty'
	return box
end 'serviceTakePending'

// serviceUnderLoad is whether the service whose message the green thread runs slows its senders, as a send to it would
// find; off a message it is false.
function serviceUnderLoad() returns bool
	let context = __Raw.threadContext()
	let g = __Raw.loadWord(context, offset: contextGreenOffset)
	if g == noGreen 'off'
		return false
	end 'off'
	let svc = messageOf(g)
	if svc == noService 'task'
		return false
	end 'task'
	let gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(svc, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	return gate < serviceClosed and (gate and serviceSlowing) >= turnBehaviours
end 'serviceUnderLoad'

// Shedding (README, "Overload policies"). Every behaviour of a service whose type has kinds of a SheddingPolicy holds a mark
// word as its first capture (markOffset): which of the type's shedding policies its kind names, times markPolicyUnit, plus
// markShed once a policy sheds it; its second is its case of the type's message union, so that from it on its captures are a
// value of the union, which a view lends where it lies (messageOffset). Such a service
// has one word after its pending words, its horizon: the newest message the last views were given, or 0. A message's start
// (serviceShedStart) is where the policies' shed is due: while the service is behind and the messages of the last views have
// all started, so each message is in at most one view. A view (serviceViewOpen) is the messages of one policy's kinds from the
// one that starts up to the horizon, the tail of the queue the first view of a start found, and a message a policy marked
// runs its behaviour's thunk all the same, which drops what it holds instead of running the handler. A service whose type
// has no shedding kind holds no mark and no horizon, and its messages start as they did.
let shedRun = 0 as Count

let shedDropped = 1 as Count

let shedDue = 2 as Count

let markShed = 1 as Count

let markPolicyUnit = 0x100000000 as Count

let noHorizon = 0 as Count

function markOffset() returns Count
	return cownOffset(1, index: 1)
end 'markOffset'

// messageOffset is where a behaviour's message, its case and its arguments, starts: after its mark.
function messageOffset() returns Count
	return __Raw.wrappingAdd(markOffset(), right: wordBytes)
end 'messageOffset'

// runningRecord is the record of the behaviour the green thread runs.
function runningRecord() returns Count
	return __Raw.loadWord(__Raw.loadWord(__Raw.threadContext(), offset: contextGreenOffset), offset: greenArgOffset)
end 'runningRecord'

function markedShed(record Count) returns Count
	if (__Raw.loadWord(record, offset: markOffset()) and markShed) != 0 'shed'
		return shedDropped
	end 'shed'
	return shedRun
end 'markedShed'

// serviceShedStart is serviceStart of a message of a type with shedding kinds, and says what becomes of it: it runs, it was
// shed by a view it was in, the policies' shed is due first, or its service was stopped at once (shedStopped). Only the
// service's holder reads and writes the horizon.
function serviceShedStart(state Count, slot Count) returns Count
	serviceStart(state)
	let handle = __Raw.wrappingSub(state, right: cownValueOffset)
	if stoppedNow(handle) 'stopped'
		// A service stopped at once never sheds again, so its horizon is never read.
		return shedStopped
	end 'stopped'
	let record = runningRecord()
	let horizon = __Raw.loadWord(handle, offset: pendingOffset(slot))
	if horizon == record 'last'
		__Raw.storeWord(handle, offset: pendingOffset(slot), value: noHorizon)
		return markedShed(record)
	end 'last'
	if horizon != noHorizon 'seen'
		return markedShed(record)
	end 'seen'
	let gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	if (gate and serviceBehind) != 0 or (gate and serviceQueued) >= turnBehaviours 'behind'
		return shedDue
	end 'behind'
	return markedShed(record)
end 'serviceShedStart'

function serviceShedMarked(state Count) returns bool
	return markedShed(runningRecord()) == shedDropped
end 'serviceShedMarked'

// serviceViewOpen is the view of one shedding policy, an array of the messages of its kinds, from the one that starts to the
// horizon, which the first view of the start sets to the tail of the queue: each element is a value of the message union,
// the words of its behaviour from its case on, which the array lends. A message behind the tail that has exchanged itself
// into it may not have linked yet, so the walk waits for each link, as a release does.
function serviceViewOpen(state Count, slot Count, policy Count) returns Count
	let handle = __Raw.wrappingSub(state, right: cownValueOffset)
	let first = runningRecord()
	var last = __Raw.loadWord(handle, offset: pendingOffset(slot))
	if last == noHorizon 'tail'
		last = __Raw.loadWord(__Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: cownTailOffset), offset: requestOwnerOffset)
		__Raw.storeWord(handle, offset: pendingOffset(slot), value: last)
	end 'tail'
	var n = 0 as Count
	var record = first
	while true 'count'
		if policyOfMark(record) == policy 'mine'
			n = __Raw.wrappingAdd(n, right: 1)
		end 'mine'
		if record == last 'end'
			break 'count'
		end 'end'
		record = recordAfter(record)
	end 'count'
	let view = arrayNew()
	var data = 0 as Count
	if n > 0 'some'
		data = alloc(try n * wordBytes otherwise panic("a view past memory"))
	end 'some'
	__Raw.storeWord(view, offset: arrayLengthOffset, value: n)
	__Raw.storeWord(view, offset: arrayCapacityOffset, value: n)
	__Raw.storeWord(view, offset: arrayDataOffset, value: data)
	var at = 0 as Count
	record = first
	while true 'fill'
		if policyOfMark(record) == policy 'mine'
			__Raw.storeWord(data, offset: at, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(record, right: messageOffset()))
			at = __Raw.wrappingAdd(at, right: wordBytes)
		end 'mine'
		if record == last 'end'
			break 'fill'
